 soluble vitamins are :<br />A. Vitamin $B_1$<br />B. Vitamin C<br />C. Vitamin E<br />D. Vitamin $\\mathrm{B}_{12}$<br />E. Vitamin K<br />Choose the correct answer from the options given below :"}, "options": [{"id": "e19dc3", "text": "C & D Only", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f9a2ee0e063d92a72d3"}, {"id": "44830b", "text": "A & B Only", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f9a2ee0e063d92a72d4"}, {"id": "d8efc9", "text": "B & C Only", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f9a2ee0e063d92a72d5"}, {"id": "e5da03", "text": "C & E Only", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f9a2ee0e063d92a72d6"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f9a2ee0e063d92a72d7", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60ced5dd19d2aa2b78a3a78c"], "topics": ["60e40fd6f1ef260f84ed1ea3"], "concepts": ["6117b89067dbe2603c3e80c4"], "question": {"text": "Which of the following statement is not true for glucose?"}, "options": [{"id": "e6155c", "text": "Glucose exists in two crystalline forms <math><mi>α</mi></math> and <math><mi>β</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f9a2ee0e063d92a72d8"}, {"id": "53a706", "text": "Glucose gives Schiff’s test for aldehyde", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f9a2ee0e063d92a72d9"}, {"id": "9b21c0", "text": "Glucose reacts with hydroxylamine to form oxime", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f9a2ee0e063d92a72da"}, {"id": "c05a31", "text": "The pentaacetate of glucose does not react with hydroxylamine to give oxime", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f9a2ee0e063d92a72db"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f9a2ee0e063d92a72dc", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60ced5dd19d2aa2b78a3a78c"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b4775"], "concepts": ["60e2fad966f9ce3f243b472b"], "question": {"text": "Two nucleotides are joined together by a linkage known as :"}, "options": [{"id": "6908c1", "text": "Phosphodiester linkage", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f9a2ee0e063d92a72dd"}, {"id": "64161b", "text": "Glycosidic linkage", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f9a2ee0e063d92a72de"}, {"id": "ba8dfb", "text": "Disulphide linkage", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f9a2ee0e063d92a72df"}, {"id": "8535bb", "text": "Peptide linkage ", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f9a2ee0e063d92a72e0"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f9a2ee0e063d92a72e1", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60ced5dd19d2aa2b78a3a78c"], "topics": ["60e2fb5766f9ce3f243b4f43"], "concepts": ["60e2fb5666f9ce3f243b4f16"], "question": {"text": "The naturally occurring amino acid that contains only one basic functional group in its chemical structure is"}, "options": [{"id": "3e7da7", "text": "asparagine", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f9a2ee0e063d92a72e2"}, {"id": "b5e1d6", "text": "histidine", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f9a2ee0e063d92a72e3"}, {"id": "b445fa", "text": "arginine", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f9a2ee0e063d92a72e4"}, {"id": "6a59bf", "text": "lysine", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f9a2ee0e063d92a72e5"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f9a2ee0e063d92a72e6", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60ced5dd19d2aa2b78a3a78c"], "topics": ["60e40fd6f1ef260f84ed1ea2"], "concepts": ["60e5678c499d1e49588f9265"], "question": {"text": "<p>Given below are two statements. One is labelled as Assertion <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math> and the other is labelled as Reason <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi></math>.</p><p>Assertion <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math> : Amylose is insoluble in water.</p><p>Reason <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi></math> : Amylose is a long linear molecule with more than <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>200</mn></math> glucose units.</p><p>In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below.</p>"}, "options": [{"id": "ca0911", "text": "Both <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi></math> are correct and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi></math> is the correct explanation of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math>.", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f9a2ee0e063d92a72e7"}, {"id": "151514", "text": "Both <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi></math> are correct and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi></math> is NOT the correct explanation of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math>.", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f9a2ee0e063d92a72e8"}, {"id": "7a6ec8", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math> is correct but&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi></math>&nbsp;is not correct.", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f9a2ee0e063d92a72e9"}, {"id": "26247f", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math> is not correct but <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi></math> is correct.", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f9a2ee0e063d92a72ea"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f9a2ee0e063d92a72eb", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60ced5dd19d2aa2b78a3a78c"], "topics": ["60e2fbdc66f9ce3f243b5674"], "concepts": ["63b037db57d0ee3c85318a16"], "question": {"text": "The one that does not stabilize <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mn>2</mn><mo>&#8728;</mo></msup></math>&nbsp;and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mn>3</mn><mo>&#8728;</mo></msup></math> structures of proteins is"}, "options": [{"id": "f1a5b1", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8211;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mo>&#8211;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mo>&#8211;</mo></math>linkage", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f9a2ee0e063d92a72ec"}, {"id": "3a2fdd", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mo>-</mo></math>bonding", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f9a2ee0e063d92a72ed"}, {"id": "8b7b7b", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8211;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mo>&#8211;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mo>&#8211;</mo></math>linkage", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f9a2ee0e063d92a72ee"}, {"id": "595042", "text": "van der Waals forces", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f9a2ee0e063d92a72ef"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f9a2ee0e063d92a72f0", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60ced5dd19d2aa2b78a3a78c"], "topics": ["60e567ac499d1e49588f95eb"], "concepts": ["6117b64d478ae845c43c685c"], "question": {"text": "Which among the following pairs of Vitamins is stored in our body relatively for longer duration?"}, "options": [{"id": "17c8b1", "text": "Thiamine and Vitamin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f9a2ee0e063d92a72f1"}, {"id": "6b45b4", "text": "Vitamin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f9a2ee0e063d92a72f2"}, {"id": "773e77", "text": "Thiamine and Ascorbic acid", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f9a2ee0e063d92a72f3"}, {"id": "00b540", "text": "Ascorbic acid and Vitamin&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D</mi></math>&nbsp;", "isCorrect": null, 